      "assistant_text": "## Answer\n\nCredential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are closely related \u2014 they both apply the same architectural principle.\n\n**The Connection:**\n\nBoth [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] centralize environment setup at a single composition point at startup, rather than letting each component discover its configuration independently.\n\n- **Credential Scoping** centralizes credential injection: the Hub\u0027s secrets loader reads the API key once from the \u0060.env\u0060 file and injects it into only the child process that will use it. No agent has to rediscover how to access the credential.\n\n- **Runtime Paths** centralizes path resolution: a single path resolver at startup reads configuration (command line, environment, config file, defaults), validates everything exists, and vends the complete resolved path set. No downstream subsystem independently rediscovers locations from the working directory.\n\n**Why this pattern matters:**\n\n1. **Prevents distributed logic** \u2014 each configuration concern is resolved once, not reimplemented across the codebase.\n2. **Single point of control** \u2014 the Hub can verify setup is correct before vending it downstream.\n3. **Reduced attack surface** \u2014 configuration sources are consulted only at the composition point, not scattered where they might be mishandled.\n4. **Testability** \u2014 tests can provide a fixed set of credentials and paths without mocking environment variables in multiple places.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], which recognizes this as a deliberate architectural pattern worth understanding and extending consistently across other configuration concerns.",
